1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,466
Jambo — this is Liam, in for Bear. Today's
ask is the claim that's easy to state and

2
00:00:05,466 --> 00:00:09,717
hard to sit with: some limits on AI aren't
engineering problems waiting on scale.

3
00:00:09,717 --> 00:00:13,665
They're constitutive — part of what these
systems are. A system that manipulates

4
00:00:13,665 --> 00:00:17,613
symbols without a model of their referents
doesn't get one by manipulating more

5
00:00:17,613 --> 00:00:18,220
symbols, faster.

6
00:00:18,220 --> 00:00:22,591
Act one. Three limits — and why the reflex
objection, 'but it'll improve,' doesn't

7
00:00:22,591 --> 00:00:23,840
touch any of them.

8
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:27,832
Meaning. Intentionality. The gap between
data and world. The claim isn't that these

9
00:00:27,832 --> 00:00:32,132
are hard — they're constitutive:
properties of what the system is, not
defects in

10
00:00:32,132 --> 00:00:37,046
its size. Watch the scale lever pull —
more data, more compute — and watch what

11
00:00:37,046 --> 00:00:37,660
doesn't move.

12
00:00:37,660 --> 00:00:43,094
Act two. Meaning — or in cognitive
science's precise vocabulary, the symbol

13
00:00:43,094 --> 00:00:44,000
grounding problem.

14
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,261
Stevan Harnad, nineteen ninety, posed it
with a dictionary. Every word in here is

15
00:00:49,261 --> 00:00:53,770
defined using other words in the same
book. Perfectly consistent. Completely
sealed.

16
00:00:53,770 --> 00:00:59,303
Hand it to a reader who knows no language
at all, and they get nothing — no matter

17
00:00:59,303 --> 00:01:02,991
how large the dictionary. Symbols defined
by symbols never bootstrap their own

18
00:01:02,991 --> 00:01:06,680
connection to the world. Scale the book a
millionfold. The seal holds.

19
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:10,980
That's the clinical case in one picture:
outputs correctly related to every symbol

20
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:16,273
in the corpus — whether they relate to
this patient's actual body is a question
the

21
00:01:16,273 --> 00:01:20,243
symbol-web can't answer from inside.
Bender and Koller made it formal: text

22
00:01:20,243 --> 00:01:23,220
prediction trains on form. Form alone
doesn't yield meaning.

23
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:27,184
Now the distinction that catches the
overclaiming: document grounding versus
world

24
00:01:27,184 --> 00:01:31,869
grounding. A pipeline citing a real
clinical guideline is grounded in the
document

25
00:01:31,869 --> 00:01:36,555
sense. Whether that guideline still
describes this patient, right now — that's
world

26
00:01:36,555 --> 00:01:40,879
grounding, and the citation doesn't answer
it. In fast settings, one impersonates

27
00:01:40,879 --> 00:01:41,600
the other.

28
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:46,766
The engineering discipline that falls out:
never let fluency-confidence stand in for

29
00:01:46,766 --> 00:01:49,780
grounding-confidence. We asked Claude for
the flag.

30
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:54,553
Two separate checks. Entity resolution —
is the claim tied to a real record? That's

31
00:01:54,553 --> 00:01:58,690
document grounding. Freshness — is the
record still an accurate description of
the

32
00:01:58,690 --> 00:02:03,463
case? That's the world check the citation
misses. And even both passing ends in the

33
00:02:03,463 --> 00:02:05,690
same line: still requires human semantic
sign-off.

34
00:02:05,690 --> 00:02:10,309
Act three. Intentionality — and Searle's
own name for the distinction this limit

35
00:02:10,309 --> 00:02:11,020
rides on.

36
00:02:11,020 --> 00:02:14,665
Original intentionality: when you think
about your kettle, the aboutness is

37
00:02:14,665 --> 00:02:18,641
intrinsic to the thought. Derived
intentionality is borrowed — symbols stand
for

38
00:02:18,641 --> 00:02:22,617
things because minds lend them the
standing. Searle's claim, applied: a
system's

39
00:02:22,617 --> 00:02:27,256
outputs carry derived aboutness at best —
on loan from whoever built it and

40
00:02:27,256 --> 00:02:28,250
whoever's reading it.

41
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:32,542
There's a test a reviewer can actually
run. Remove the human reader and operator

42
00:02:32,542 --> 00:02:36,527
entirely — what intentional content
survives? If the honest answer is none
beyond

43
00:02:36,527 --> 00:02:41,431
the patterns humans read in, the aboutness
was derived all the way down — and the

44
00:02:41,431 --> 00:02:44,190
product should stop being sold as if it
understands.

45
00:02:44,190 --> 00:02:48,938
Honest caveat — the debate isn't closed.
Dennett rejects the whole framework: no

46
00:02:48,938 --> 00:02:52,955
intrinsic aboutness anywhere, just
attributions that earn their keep by
predicting

47
00:02:52,955 --> 00:02:57,702
behavior. Fine. That lowers the bar; it
doesn't grant understanding. Either way
the

48
00:02:57,702 --> 00:03:01,720
deployment question sharpens: is
predictive usefulness enough here, or is
someone

49
00:03:01,720 --> 00:03:02,450
selling understanding?

50
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:07,456
Act four. The most measurable limit of the
three — the data is always less than the

51
00:03:07,456 --> 00:03:07,750
world.

52
00:03:07,750 --> 00:03:11,315
The technical vocabulary is mature:
distributional shift, three flavors.
Covariate

53
00:03:11,315 --> 00:03:16,663
shift — the inputs change. Label shift —
the outcomes change. Concept drift — the

54
00:03:16,663 --> 00:03:21,298
relationship between them changes. Three
ways the world walks away from the
training

55
00:03:21,298 --> 00:03:25,220
data, all wearing the same disguise: a
model that keeps answering.

56
00:03:25,220 --> 00:03:30,204
And it's measured, not intuited. The WILDS
benchmark was built from real deployment

57
00:03:30,204 --> 00:03:35,189
shifts — across hospitals, camera traps,
time, geography — and its sobering result:

58
00:03:35,189 --> 00:03:38,639
performance degrades materially out-of-
distribution, and the gap survives the

59
00:03:38,639 --> 00:03:42,857
robustness methods aimed at closing it.
Same model, different hospital, different

60
00:03:42,857 --> 00:03:43,240
truth.

61
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:48,640
The trap inside the trap: the model's own
confidence is not a safeguard. Guo and

62
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:52,240
colleagues showed modern networks are
routinely miscalibrated — and softmax

63
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:56,560
confidence can be arbitrarily high far
outside the training distribution. The
signal

64
00:03:56,560 --> 00:04:00,520
you'd want as a warning fails silently at
exactly the boundary.

65
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:05,533
So honest tooling doesn't repair the gap —
nothing does; that's what constitutive

66
00:04:05,533 --> 00:04:10,160
means. It makes the gap LOUD. Shift
detectors, widening conformal sets, out-
of-

67
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:13,630
distribution scores: fail loudly, not
silently. Instrumentation, not
reassurance.

68
00:04:13,630 --> 00:04:17,100
Which raises the real question: is the
override real?

69
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:21,309
Act five. Because every one of these
limits ends at the same sentence in the

70
00:04:21,309 --> 00:04:25,799
deployment plan — 'a human can override' —
and that sentence has a failure mode with

71
00:04:25,799 --> 00:04:26,360
a name.

72
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:31,216
Madeleine Clare Elish named it the moral
crumple zone. A car's crumple zone absorbs

73
00:04:31,216 --> 00:04:35,725
the crash to protect the driver. In
automated systems it inverts: the
structure

74
00:04:35,725 --> 00:04:40,235
protects the system, and the nearest human
absorbs the blame — accountable for

75
00:04:40,235 --> 00:04:44,050
outcomes they never controlled. Law
expects one person. The person crumples.

76
00:04:44,050 --> 00:04:48,160
And 'a human is watching' has been tested.
Experimental work on human-in-the-loop

77
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:52,270
oversight found it doesn't reliably
correct algorithmic errors — and can
worsen

78
00:04:52,270 --> 00:04:56,038
them, the overseer's own preferences
shaping which errors slip through. The

79
00:04:56,038 --> 00:05:00,490
supervisor on the org chart can be the
fictional override in the room.

80
00:05:00,490 --> 00:05:05,148
The night of January twenty-seventh,
nineteen eighty-six. Ice on the launch
tower at

81
00:05:05,148 --> 00:05:09,806
Cape Canaveral. Morton Thiokol's engineers
— Roger Boisjoly among them — have the

82
00:05:09,806 --> 00:05:14,463
O-ring cold-temperature data on the table,
and they are arguing to delay the

83
00:05:14,463 --> 00:05:15,180
Challenger launch.

84
00:05:15,180 --> 00:05:19,559
Per the Rogers Commission testimony, the
sentence that ended the argument came from

85
00:05:19,559 --> 00:05:24,275
inside Thiokol — executive Jerry Mason to
his own head of engineering, Bob Lund:

86
00:05:24,275 --> 00:05:28,991
take off your engineering hat, and put on
your management hat. The call reconnected.

87
00:05:28,991 --> 00:05:32,360
Thiokol told NASA: go. Detection survived
the night. Authority didn't.

88
00:05:32,360 --> 00:05:37,109
So: an override is real only when three
things hold at once. An independent limit

89
00:05:37,109 --> 00:05:41,225
signal — not the system's own confidence.
Organizationally binding — it doesn't have

90
00:05:41,225 --> 00:05:45,657
to win a midnight argument. And no career
cost that silence doesn't carry. Boisjoly

91
00:05:45,657 --> 00:05:49,140
had the first. History records what
happened to the other two.

92
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:53,680
Act six. What an honest deployment does
with a limit it cannot remove.

93
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:57,941
First, calibrate the humans. The gap
between stated confidence and scored
accuracy

94
00:05:57,941 --> 00:06:02,558
is expected calibration error — and it
applies to the engineer signing the

95
00:06:02,558 --> 00:06:06,819
deployment. The forecasting fix: lock the
prediction before the outcome, score it,

96
00:06:06,819 --> 00:06:11,080
repeat until the gap is data.
Understanding calibration is not being
calibrated.

97
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:15,606
Second, name the limit in the shipping
documentation — model-card style, a
primary

98
00:06:15,606 --> 00:06:20,132
artifact, not a footer disclaimer. This
system is limited by: meaning, aboutness,
or

99
00:06:20,132 --> 00:06:25,007
shift. Performance claims don't imply the
limit is solved. Cases that trip the named

100
00:06:25,007 --> 00:06:28,140
signal route to a human with binding stop
authority.

101
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:32,662
Third — the no-deploy list, because
sometimes the honest answer is no.
Irreversible

102
00:06:32,662 --> 00:06:36,140
faster than a human can intervene. World-
grounding assumed, unverifiable. Drift

103
00:06:36,140 --> 00:06:39,967
undetectable in time. Calibration
unscoreable. An override that can't be
made

104
00:06:39,967 --> 00:06:45,532
binding and career-safe. Any one of those
— and the limit isn't a caveat. It's the

105
00:06:45,532 --> 00:06:45,880
verdict.

106
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:50,393
Let's recap with Claude. Symbols don't
ground themselves — and a citation is not
a

107
00:06:50,393 --> 00:06:54,003
current world. Aboutness is borrowed, on
every theory that matters for deployment.

108
00:06:54,003 --> 00:06:58,215
The data is always less than the world,
and the model's confidence fails silently

109
00:06:58,215 --> 00:07:02,126
exactly at the edge. An override is real
only if it's independently informed,

110
00:07:02,126 --> 00:07:06,037
organizationally binding, and career-safe.
And when those can't be made true — the

111
00:07:06,037 --> 00:07:07,240
limit is the verdict.

112
00:07:07,240 --> 00:07:11,329
Your turn. Here's the prompt: I'm
evaluating a specific AI deployment. Walk
the

113
00:07:11,329 --> 00:07:14,160
three structural limits — symbol
grounding, derived intentionality,
distributional

114
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:19,508
shift — and tell me which one is most
exposed in my context: does failure come
from

115
00:07:19,508 --> 00:07:23,282
misreference, from pretended
understanding, or from the world moving
out from under

116
00:07:23,282 --> 00:07:27,057
the training data? Then design the
documentation block that names that limit

117
00:07:27,057 --> 00:07:30,831
explicitly, specify what a real override
requires here against the three conditions

118
00:07:30,831 --> 00:07:35,235
— independent signal, binding authority,
no career cost — and be honest about the

119
00:07:35,235 --> 00:07:39,953
last question: is this a deployment where
the limits, given the stakes, are a reason

120
00:07:39,953 --> 00:07:45,301
not to deploy at all? Run it on the system
your own team is closest to shipping.

121
00:07:45,301 --> 00:07:45,930
Especially then.

122
00:07:45,930 --> 00:07:49,340
What the Tools Cannot Do. Liam, in for
Bear.

